-- Example: schedule the billing watchdog.
--
-- Pure SQL, no app in the way: the function lives in the database
-- (99_supabase_migration_billing_watchdog_v1.sql) and cron calls it directly,
-- so it still speaks when the app is down or refuses the billing jobs. Every
-- 15 minutes it:
--   - posts each billing problem the app wrote (platform_audit_events,
--     billing.problem: two live subscriptions on one property, a code used past
--     its limit, a new card declined on an overdue subscription, a property
--     above 500 rooms, a missing BILLING_CRON_SECRET or STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET,
--     MAYA's copy of a subscription found out of date, ...), at most once per
--     problem per 6 hours;
--   - once Stripe billing is in use, says when a billing job has stopped
--     reporting runs: the card check after 2 hours, room-count truing and the
--     nightly Stripe check after 26 hours. One line per job while it stays
--     stopped (no repeat within 6 hours), one recovery line when it runs again.
-- Critical only by default; the second argument 'warn' sends warnings too.
--
-- Prereqs: extensions pg_cron and pg_net; the alert webhook in Vault under
-- maya_alert_webhook. That is the same secret the pricing watchdog uses
-- (supabase/cron/pricing-watchdog-every-10-min.sql.example step 1), so if the
-- pricing watchdog is scheduled there is nothing to add. Without it the
-- watchdog says so on Pilot health ("Alerts: missing. billing-watchdog said
-- ...") and records no alert.
--
-- 0) Ensure the extensions exist:
--    create extension if not exists pg_cron;
--    create extension if not exists pg_net;
--
-- 1) The webhook in Vault (skip if the pricing watchdog already has it):
--    select vault.create_secret('https://hooks.slack.com/services/...', 'maya_alert_webhook');
--
-- 2) Schedule (every 15 minutes, at :07, :22, :37, :52, after the card check
--    at :02, :17, :32, :47):
--    select cron.schedule(
--      'billing-watchdog',
--      '7,22,37,52 * * * *',
--      $$ select public.billing_watchdog(true, 'critical'); $$
--    );
--
-- Inspect:    select jobid, jobname, schedule, active from cron.job;
-- Runs log:   select * from cron.job_run_details order by start_time desc limit 20;
-- Unschedule: select cron.unschedule('billing-watchdog');
--
-- What it would say right now, without posting or recording anything:
--   select kind, alert_key, severity, title, last_at, action, detail
--     from public.billing_watchdog(false);
--
-- What it has posted (the same rows raiseAlert writes):
--   select event_type, entity_id, detail, created_at
--     from public.platform_audit_events
--    where event_type in ('alert.raised', 'alert.recovered')
--      and (entity_id like 'billing%' or entity_id like 'stripe-webhook%')
--    order by created_at desc limit 20;
--
-- The problems the app wrote, newest first:
--   select entity_id, hotel_id, detail->>'title' as title, detail->>'detail' as detail, created_at
--     from public.platform_audit_events
--    where event_type = 'billing.problem'
--    order by created_at desc limit 20;
